memory: name the guest address before the host fault

Under DIRECT_ADDRESSING a guest pointer becomes a host pointer by adding
an offset, so a guest address that has gone wrong reads host memory
belonging to something else. On Linux that is a SIGSEGV with a
backtrace. On a bare-metal port it is a data abort whose registers hold
only the host address, and the guest address — the one that would say
which piece of Mac code is at fault — is gone by then. Sometimes there is
no fault at all and a byte quietly changes.

do_get_real_address() is the single place the translation happens and the
last place the guest address exists. One compare, against a limit the
port sets, calling a reporter the port defines; the access still goes
ahead afterwards, so nothing is hidden and nothing behaves differently.
DIRECT_ADDRESSING_GUARD undefined — which is every existing build —
compiles the whole thing away.

mihaip/macemu puts an equivalent test in sysdeps.h's do_get_mem_*
instead (branch infinite-mac-kanjitalk755). That is four functions in six
variants, it prints from the hot path, and by then the address it has is
the host one.

diff --git a/BasiliskII/src/uae_cpu_2021/memory.h b/BasiliskII/src/uae_cpu_2021/memory.h
index f7bab41d..c30c0794 100644
--- a/BasiliskII/src/uae_cpu_2021/memory.h
+++ b/BasiliskII/src/uae_cpu_2021/memory.h
@@ -56,8 +56,29 @@ extern void Exception (int, uaecptr);
 #endif /* EXCEPTIONS_VIA_LONGJMP */
 
 #if DIRECT_ADDRESSING
+
+#if DIRECT_ADDRESSING_GUARD
+/* Direct addressing turns any guest address into a host one by adding an
+   offset, so a guest pointer that has gone wrong reads or writes host memory
+   that belongs to something else. On a hosted platform that is a SIGSEGV with a
+   backtrace; on a bare-metal one it is a data abort whose registers say nothing
+   about which *guest* address caused it, or — worse — no fault at all.
+
+   This is the one place where the translation happens, and the only place that
+   still has the guest address in hand. The port defines the two symbols below:
+   the size of the block it mapped, and what to say about an address outside it.
+   The access still goes ahead, so nothing is masked; the fault is merely named
+   before it happens. Costs one compare, and is compiled out otherwise. */
+extern uae_u32 direct_addressing_limit;
+extern void direct_addressing_fault(uaecptr addr);
+#endif
+
 static __inline__ uae_u8 *do_get_real_address(uaecptr addr)
 {
+#if DIRECT_ADDRESSING_GUARD
+	if (direct_addressing_limit && addr >= direct_addressing_limit)
+		direct_addressing_fault(addr);
+#endif
 	return (uae_u8 *)MEMBaseDiff + addr;
 }
 static __inline__ uae_u32 do_get_virtual_address(uae_u8 *addr)
